 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Tue, 25 Aug 2026 06:59:00 -0400

krauken (0.1.28) stable; urgency=medium

  * Removed the cascade's ramp-feedforward term entirely -- confirmed
    live to be a real bug, not just a tuning nuisance: for Free Rise's
    authored rate (1.5F/h), the feedforward term alone exceeded the
    entire chamber safety envelope, pinning chamber_target_f at 90F for
    over 3 of the stage's ~4 hours while beer overshot the ramp's own end
    target by 3F. The term was carried over from the old bang-bang
    cascade, which had no integral state at all and genuinely needed a
    manual substitute for one -- with a real PI controller, it's
    redundant: standard control theory says the integral term settles,
    on its own, at exactly the value needed to sustain zero steady-state
    error against a constant-rate ramp, no separate term required, held
    stage or ramping one, treated identically. chamber_target_for() now
    takes no ramp-rate input at all.
  * Re-derived INTEGRAL_MAX_F_H (3.0 -> 4.0 F-h) now that it's the only
    thing governing both exotherm cancellation and ramp-tracking --
    sized with margin over the two real, already-authored disturbances
    that matter (the documented peak exotherm, and Cold Crash's own
    ramp), not the largest rate anyone could ever author. A ramp faster
    than that (Free Rise's own 1.5F/h) now legitimately, safely lags
    instead of saturating the envelope -- surfaced honestly via the
    dashboard's "won't reach target" state rather than silently blown
    through.
  * Removed contracts/stages.py's now-fully-unused target_rate_f_per_h().

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 21:54:51 -0400

krauken (0.1.27) stable; urgency=medium

  * Dashboard: replaced the Setpoint tile's "±0.5°F" sublabel -- a
    hardcoded literal never actually derived from anything the cascade
    does, and actively misleading once the PI controller can legitimately
    push the chamber far from the beer target on purpose (a ramping
    stage's feedforward) -- with a projected time-to-target estimate
    ("2h to target", "<1h to target", or "Won't reach target" when the
    beer is projected to keep trailing a moving ramp target for the rest
    of the stage). Reuses the existing forward-projection (already a full
    PI-cascade re-simulation, not a naive "hold today's setpoint forever"
    calc) -- same simulator-calibrated coefficients the chart's projection
    has always used for real hardware, a prior, permanent decision, not a
    new imprecision this introduces. Only shown for the live/current
    state, not while scrubbing the chart through history (the projection
    is anchored to "now").

krauken (0.1.26) stable; urgency=medium

  * Round the chamber target to 1 decimal place before writing it to the
    BrewPi Arduino -- confirmed live via the serial trace log that the PI
    cascade's raw output (the full accumulated-integral float, e.g.
    52.672244164987845) was going out over the wire verbatim every tick,
    bloating each `j` write for digits the Arduino's own temperature
    resolution can't meaningfully use. Rounding before the existing
    dedupe check (v0.1.23) is a double win, not just a smaller payload:
    the PI integral nudges its raw output by a tiny fraction almost every
    tick even while genuinely holding steady, so rounding first also
    means the dedupe matches far more often, cutting real `j` writes
    outright, not just their length.

krauken (0.1.25) stable; urgency=medium

  * Replaced the beer-holding cascade's fixed-clamp bang-bang scheme with
    a PI controller on beer-temp error (no gravity dependency). Confirmed
    two real bugs the old scheme had via live fermentation data: (1) a
    sustained deviation (beer running warm the whole 12h Primary stage)
    never got more than the same fixed 5F cooling clamp, no matter how
    long it persisted -- the "long idle then a spike" pattern that
    originally motivated this; (2) a genuine, confirmed sign bug in the
    old ramp-feedforward math (abs(ramp_rate)/coupling, widening whichever
    discrete cool/heat/idle mode the hysteresis happened to be in, with no
    check that the ramp's own direction agreed) caused chamber_target_f to
    swing between ~53F and ~80F almost every tick during the Free Rise
    stage's 66->70F ramp, exactly reproduced and confirmed from the real
    samples table this session. The new controller has no discrete mode at
    all: contracts/cascade.py's chamber_target_for()/
    update_beer_error_integral() compute a continuous
    beer_target + Kp*error + Ki*integral + signed_ramp_feedforward,
    anti-windup clamped, replacing beer_relay_demand() entirely.
  * New, dedicated test suite (test_cascade_setpoint_stability.py) proves
    the fix directly against the real incident data: replays the exact
    recorded sequence that broke the old cascade and confirms zero
    cooling<->heating reversals under the new one (verified the metric
    itself is meaningful by replaying the same data through the OLD
    cascade logic and confirming it DOES flag a reversal there), plus
    synthetic sensor-noise-chatter, dramatic-movement-is-fine, and
    ramp-direction-consistency scenarios.

krauken (0.1.24) stable; urgency=medium

  * Hardware Setup: removed the standalone "Test heater" button
    (confirm_heater) -- the guided wizard now discovers and installs the
    heat pin itself (device_config.py's identify_relay_pin), so this
    separate post-setup diagnostic was redundant and confusing next to it.
  * Hardware Setup: finishing the wizard now triggers a real rescan
    (the same job the mount effect and "Scan again" run) instead of just
    invalidating the already-cached devices query -- that cached query
    reflects whatever the LAST scan wrote, which predates whatever the
    wizard just changed on real hardware. Fixes a real, confirmed bug: a
    newly-installed beer_temp probe's "roles it plays" chip stayed
    invisible until the next unrelated scan (a page reload, or a manual
    "Scan again" click).

krauken (0.1.23) stable; urgency=medium

  * Dedupe redundant BrewPi `j` writes: set_fridge_target() used to write
    and burst-drain a real serial `j{...}` command every ~30s control-loop
    tick even when the target hadn't changed (a held/constant fermentation
    stage keeps it bit-identical tick to tick). It now skips the write
    entirely when the requested temp already matches the Arduino's own
    most-recently-reported FridgeSet (from a real `T:` response) --
    grounded in a live read rather than a locally-cached assumption, so
    it stays correct across any reset (including `reset_brewpi`, which is
    deliberately allowed to run during an active fermentation) with no
    separate invalidation bookkeeping: whatever the Arduino reports after
    a reset is exactly what the next comparison uses.
  * Persist & display the real chamber target, not the beer target: the
    dashboard's "Setpoint" tile and the chart's "Setpoint" line both used
    to show effective_target_f (the beer target) a second time under a
    different label -- there was no distinct chamber-side value flowing
    anywhere past control_loop.py, even though it's computed every tick
    (chamber_target_for()) and simply discarded after being handed to the
    chamber driver. New `samples.chamber_target_f` column, threaded through
    the control loop, live-state payload, series/projection API responses,
    and the simulator's forward-projection preview; the frontend now reads
    this distinct value for the Setpoint tile and chart line.

krauken (0.1.22) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: set_fridge_target() (every `j{...}` write to the BrewPi Arduino)
    used to write and return without reading anything back. Confirmed via
    the new serial trace log plus the real firmware source
    (PiLink::receiveJson() in brewpi-remix/brewpi-firmware-rmx) that a
    'j' write always answers with a variable-length burst -- 0+ D:/T:
    lines (one D: per settings key, plus a full extra annotated T: line
    for each of mode/fridgeSet that actually changed), then always S:
    then always C: last, regardless of body validity. Left unread, that
    burst sat in the serial buffer until whatever LATER, unrelated
    _query_locked() call happened to run next -- which matches on prefix
    alone with no freshness check, so it could (and did, confirmed live)
    return a stale T: a 'j' had queued up well before the 't' that
    "answered" it was ever sent. set_fridge_target() now drains and
    discards the full burst (via a new _drain_until_locked() helper,
    reading until a C: line arrives or QUERY_TIMEOUT_S elapses) before
    returning, so nothing from a 'j' write can leak into and contaminate
    a later, unrelated read. Purely this one write path for now -- the
    still-open question of unrelated async D: messages splicing into the
    MIDDLE of a response (separate from this) is intentionally left
    unmitigated pending another look at the trace log with this fixed.

krauken (0.1.21) stable; urgency=medium

  * Added a raw serial read/write trace log for the BrewPi connection
    (krauken/platforms/brewpi/connection.py) -- every byte in or out now
    goes through one of two wrapper methods that log an ISO-timestamped
    (millisecond precision, UTC) READ or WRITE line to its own rotating
    file, /var/lib/krauken/brewpi-serial.log. This is purely diagnostic:
    the control loop has been observed on a real rig stalling for real
    minutes at a time (well beyond the ~15s worst case a single bad
    serial read should cause), and OG auto-detection never locking on a
    live fermentation was traced to exactly this -- readings arriving too
    sparsely for its stability window to ever see a gap-free span, not a
    detection-logic bug. Rather than guess further at the mechanism
    (one confirmed cause so far: an Arduino log message spliced into a
    temperature-read response), this makes the next occurrence directly
    readable instead of inferred. Lazy, best-effort setup (never crashes
    real serial I/O over a logging concern; harmlessly no-ops on a
    machine without /var/lib/krauken, e.g. the test suite).

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 19:07:26 -0400

krauken (0.1.20) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: finalize_device_config could permanently leave the real cooling
    or heating relay installed on RELAY_IDENTIFY_SLOT (15) -- the relay
    sweep's own scratch slot, meant only for testing pins during the
    wizard. Root cause: identify_relay_pin always installs every
    candidate it tests as CHAMBER_HEAT regardless of its eventual real
    role, reusing slot 15 across the whole sweep. Whichever candidate the
    sweep last confirmed as the REAL heat pin was therefore already
    sitting at slot 15 with a matching pin+function by the time finalize
    ran, so its "_slot_for() reuses a matching existing device's slot"
    heuristic picked slot 15 right back up as that pin's permanent home.
    (Cooling never hit this, since it's tested as CHAMBER_HEAT too but
    finalized as CHAMBER_COOL -- no function match, so it always landed
    on a genuinely free slot instead.) Confirmed live this session: slot
    15 still held CHAMBER_HEAT after a completed wizard run.
    _pick_free_slot() now takes an `exclude` param, and every permanent
    install (a probe, or finalize's cool/heat) refuses to reuse slot 15
    even when it already matches by pin+function. finalize_device_config
    also now unconditionally uninstalls slot 15 as an explicit step every
    run, regardless of whether anything used it that particular time --
    a real invariant now, not an incidental side effect of avoiding
    reuse.

