krauken (0.1.15) stable; urgency=medium

  * Pin 2 is now permanently excluded from the relay-identification
    sweep (new RESERVED_RELAY_PINS in device_config.py, rejected before
    identify_relay_pin touches the connection at all; mirrored in
    HardwareWizard.tsx's candidate list so the sweep never even attempts
    it). Confirmed live on the real rig: installing pin 2 as CHAMBER_HEAT
    produced chaotic, rapidly-toggling voltage -- not a clean driven
    level, and not the merely-noisy float an unclaimed pin shows --
    consistent with Arduino Uno's D2 (one of only two hardware-interrupt
    pins) being wired to the classic shield's rotary encoder, fighting
    our actuator output for the same physical pin. The shield's own
    original wiring instructions also explicitly call out pins 5/6 as
    the intended relay pins, never 2. A denylist, not an allowlist --
    pins other than 2 aren't restricted, preserving this project's
    "test whatever the Arduino actually reports as available" discovery
    design for any other pin a different board/shield might expose.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 17:21:55 -0400

krauken (0.1.14) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: TiltScanner.start()'s aioblescan create_bt_socket() call is a
    synchronous, blocking syscall that hangs indefinitely against an
    rfkill soft-blocked or powered-off hci device instead of raising --
    confirmed live against a fresh headless Raspberry Pi OS install
    (nothing ever powers Bluetooth on automatically without a desktop's
    Bluetooth applet). Because start() ran it directly on the daemon's
    own event loop, this didn't just make Tilt unavailable: it starved
    the ENTIRE daemon -- the IPC server never got to start listening (the
    API then reported "cannot reach daemon" for every request, including
    Hardware Setup's scan), and even SIGTERM stopped being handled, since
    asyncio's own signal delivery runs on that same blocked loop
    (systemd had to SIGKILL it after a 90s stop-sigterm timeout). Fixed:
    the connect sequence now runs off the event loop via
    asyncio.to_thread and is bounded by a new TILT_START_TIMEOUT_S
    (10s) -- a timeout now converts into the same, already-handled
    PlatformUnavailable outcome Tilt startup failures always used to
    produce, instead of hanging the caller (and the whole daemon)
    forever. New regression test confirms this without ever needing a
    real (or even fake-but-fast) Bluetooth adapter.
  * Fix: Hardware Setup's scan-start mutation had no onError handler --
    if POST /hardware/scan itself failed (confirmed live: exactly the
    daemon-unreachable case above), scanId just stayed unset forever and
    the page silently sat on "Scanning for devices..." indefinitely, with
    no indication anything had gone wrong. This is a different case from
    a scan that completes with zero devices found (that already had its
    own "No devices found" empty-state row). Both the auto-start effect
    and the "Scan again" button now surface the real API error in a
    clear banner instead.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 12:17:51 -0400

krauken (0.1.13) stable; urgency=medium

  * Supply-chain security: Python dependencies were declared as loose
    lower bounds only (fastapi>=0.115, pydantic>=2.8, etc.) with no
    lockfile, and -- unlike the frontend, which npm ci's a locked
    package-lock.json -- were resolved live from PyPI/piwheels.org on the
    end user's own Pi at install time (postinst's `pip install -e
    ".[pi]"`), not at CI build time. Two installs of the identical .deb,
    weeks apart, could silently pull different actual dependency code,
    with no lockfile to even ask afterward what was really installed.
    Added requirements-pi.lock.txt (production, exact-pinned via `uv pip
    compile`) and requirements-dev.lock.txt (dev+pi, what CI's own test
    step runs against) -- postinst and debian/rules' override_dh_auto_test
    now install from these locked files (then the package itself
    editable/--no-deps) instead of resolving pyproject.toml's ranges live.
    Deliberately version-pinned, not hash-pinned: hashes are per-artifact,
    and this lock file can't be generated with piwheels' actual ARM wheel
    hashes from a dev machine -- a mismatch against whatever pip
    legitimately downloads on the real target architecture would break
    every install. Version-pinning alone already fixes the reproducibility
    problem; hash-pinning is a real follow-up that needs generating (or
    verifying) on the actual target architecture first.
  * Enabled Dependabot vulnerability alerts + automated security updates,
    and secret scanning + push protection (all free for this public
    repo, all were off). Added .github/dependabot.yml for weekly
    version-update PRs across pip, npm, and the release workflow's own
    GitHub Actions versions -- reviewed, not auto-merged, since there's
    no CI running on plain pushes/PRs yet for any of them to lean on
    besides a human actually running the suite locally.
  * Added .github/workflows/security-scan.yml (push/PR-triggered, unlike
    build-deb.yml which only runs on release tags): pip-audit against
    both lock files, npm audit --audit-level=high against the frontend.
    Running this for the first time immediately found a real, live high
    severity vulnerability (nanoid, a transitive frontend dependency) --
    fixed via `npm audit fix` in the same pass, confirmed build/lint
    still clean.
  * build-deb.yml now generates a CycloneDX SBOM for both ecosystems
    (pip-audit --format cyclonedx-json against requirements-pi.lock.txt;
    npm sbom --sbom-format cyclonedx for the frontend) and attaches both
    to the GitHub Release alongside the .deb on every real release, for
    "was version X affected by CVE-Y" traceability later.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sat, 22 Aug 2026 11:34:54 -0400

krauken (0.1.12) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: install_device() (connection.py) was a pure fire-and-forget
    write, and 0.1.11's redesigned identify_relay_pin now abandons the
    previous candidate and installs the next one back-to-back, with
    nothing else awaited in between. Confirmed live via strace of the
    daemon's own serial fd during real wizard testing: the two writes
    arrived only ~12ms apart, and the second one came back corrupted --
    echoed as a fully-zeroed/uninstalled device instead of what was
    actually sent. Installing a device almost certainly triggers a
    synchronous EEPROM write on the AVR (~3.3ms/byte, blocks the main
    loop), which can outlast the Arduino's small serial RX buffer if a
    second full command's bytes arrive before the first has been fully
    processed -- exactly what happened. The old wait-based wizard design
    never hit this: it always had several real seconds of polling between
    installs by accident; the new, deliberately-fast off-based sweep
    removed that accidental protection. install_device() now settles for
    a new INSTALL_SETTLE_S (0.3s, held under the connection's own lock so
    nothing else can slip a command into the same window) after every
    write, fixing every call site uniformly rather than requiring each
    caller to remember to pace itself. Uses a real asyncio.sleep(), not
    the injected clock -- same reasoning as identify_and_connect()'s own
    BOOT_DELAY_S: this method can run before any platform role is mapped,
    exactly when the daemon's clock selection can land on the accelerated
    SimulatorClock, which would silently defeat a clock-relative delay.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 21:13:42 -0400

krauken (0.1.11) stable; urgency=medium

  * Redesign: identify_relay_pin no longer forces a heat demand and polls
    the firmware's own State for a genuine HEATING transition -- which
    meant paying BrewPi's real anti-short-cycle wait (up to 600s,
    TempControl.h's MIN_SWITCH_TIME/MIN_COOL_OFF_TIME_FRIDGE_CONSTANT) the
    first time a relay engaged. That protection only ever guards against
    turning something ON too soon, never against turning off, so the
    action now forces the chamber OFF instead (set_fridge_target(None),
    already-existing j{mode:"o"}) and installs the candidate -- takes
    effect on the very next control-loop tick, no wait at all, regardless
    of sweep history. The only way anything visibly reacts is if the
    guessed invert is backwards for that pin's real wiring (our "off" is
    actually its "on"), which is invisible to the firmware/API -- so the
    job result no longer has an "outcome" field; every test now needs a
    human to confirm whether anything reacted, immediately after each
    (now near-instant) test. The wizard's pin sweep asks that yes/no
    directly, tries the reversed polarity on the same pin before ruling it
    out (a real device can't hide from both polarities being tried), and
    silently self-corrects a just-identified pin back to its confirmed-
    safe invert before moving to the next candidate. _safe_uninstall_heat
    (which used to check live State to decide whether a pin needed a
    flip-then-uninstall) is replaced by _abandon_heat, which takes the
    wizard's own confirmed-safe-invert bookkeeping directly instead --
    strictly more reliable than the old State-based check ever was, and
    the only option left now that State can no longer distinguish
    "correctly off" from "incorrectly energized" during identification.
    Removes the now-unused everEngaged/Skip-button gating entirely (there
    is no multi-minute wait left to skip).

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Wed, 19 Aug 2026 20:27:35 -0400

krauken (0.1.10) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: connection.py's BOOT_DELAY_S (the wait after opening a fresh
    serial connection, before querying the Arduino to identify it) was
    4.0s, based on an earlier, incorrect measurement. Confirmed live,
    extensively and repeatedly this session, that the real post-DTR-reset
    boot time on this hardware is ~15-20s -- dominated by the display
    driver's I2C address scan running with no display physically present
    to ACK. The old value meant every identify_and_connect() attempt
    queried the Arduino before it had actually finished booting, got no
    response at all, closed the connection, and logged "BrewPi not found
    at startup" -- a real, reproducible daemon bug, not a one-off,
    confirmed via the daemon's own journal on the actual rig. Now 22.0s
    (real margin over the observed worst case). Only paid on a genuine
    cold-connect (first time, or after a lost connection) -- an
    already-connected BrewPi pays nothing extra on a later rescan.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Tue, 18 Aug 2026 07:01:03 -0400

krauken (0.1.9) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: _safe_uninstall_heat (added in 0.1.8) was running on every
    candidate abandonment, but confirmed live against the real rig that
    it should only run when the pin being abandoned was actually
    confirmed as the heater or the fridge -- a pin where every polarity
    came back "nothing happened" has no confirmed off level to aim for,
    so the flip dance was just guessing. identify_relay_pin now takes an
    identified_pins param (the wizard's own confirmed heat/cool pins);
    only those get the safe-off treatment, everything else gets the
    plain bare uninstall it always got before 0.1.8.
  * Fix: reset_brewpi/_restore_baseline reinstalled the baseline's own
    devices before resetting, and only ever reinstalled what was in the
    snapshot -- never uninstalled anything else. Confirmed live: a
    currently-engaged stray device (e.g. the sweep's own scratch-slot
    candidate) stayed energized through the whole reinstall step, and
    survived a restore untouched if it wasn't part of the snapshot,
    leaving two conflicting device definitions on the same physical pin.
    The reset now happens first, and the restore reconciles against the
    current installed-device list -- uninstalling anything not in the
    snapshot -- before installing what the snapshot says should be there.
  * Fix: identify_relay_pin hard-failed the whole candidate test on a
    single missed chamber-temp read, even though the probe was confirmed
    reading fine both immediately before and after. It now retries that
    one baseline read a few times before giving up.
  * Fix: connection.py's serial query loops (_query_locked,
    _query_device_list_locked) re-sent the command on every retry
    iteration (every 0.5s, up to 4 times) instead of just waiting longer
    for a response that was simply running behind an interleaved async
    log line. Confirmed live via strace: 44 occurrences this session of
    the identical command being written twice within under a second of
    itself, and at least one case of a stale install-echo reappearing
    several minutes late and out of place -- both are the two-outstanding-
    requests problem a resend creates. The command is now written exactly
    once, with a single flat 15s wait and no resend of any kind.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Tue, 18 Aug 2026 00:14:34 -0400

krauken (0.1.8) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: identify_relay_pin's bare uninstall of whatever currently held
    CHAMBER_HEAT never turned the pin off -- confirmed live against the
    real rig: DeviceManager.cpp's uninstallDevice() only deletes the C++
    actuator object, it never calls setActive(false) first, so a pin
    that had genuinely engaged (e.g. the identified cooling relay) stayed
    electrically on indefinitely after being abandoned for the next
    candidate. New _safe_uninstall_heat helper checks whether the current
    holder is genuinely engaged right now; if so, it reinstalls the same
    pin with inverted polarity first (the still-active heat demand
    re-engages it near-instantly under the flip, driving the pin to the
    correct physical off level for its real wiring), confirms that
    re-engagement, then uninstalls for real. A candidate that was only
    ever waiting or timed out with no engagement still gets a bare
    uninstall, unchanged.
  * Fix: HardwareWizard.tsx's devicePinSweep offered a "Skip -- try the
    next test" button any time a candidate was waiting on the
    compressor-protection timer, with no regard for whether this sweep
    had ever actually engaged before -- confirmed live: skipping before
    the sweep's first genuine engagement doesn't save time, it discards
    the only real ~600s wait BrewPi's anti-short-cycle timer requires and
    forces the next candidate to pay it again from scratch, silently.
    New everEngaged state latches the first time any candidate reaches a
    real engagement; until then, no Skip button is offered at all, just
    an explanation that this first wait only happens once. Confirmed live
    this session: every subsequent candidate swap after the first real
    engagement (~2m15s) went to HEATING near-instantly, five times in a
    row, across two relays and both polarities.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 20:40:57 -0400

krauken (0.1.7) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: platforms/brewpi/device_config.py's pin-sweep (v0.1.6's
    sweep_relay) is redesigned entirely -- found live, against the real
    rig, that testing cooling and heating as two separate passes (each
    forcing its own target direction) left stray same-function candidates
    installed between passes, and BrewPi does *not* enforce
    one-actuator-per-function on its own: commanding "cooling" energized
    three separate installed CHAMBER_COOL devices at once, not just the
    intended one. Renamed to identify_relay_pin and rebuilt around a
    single invariant -- at most one device is ever installed with the
    CHAMBER_HEAT function at a time, uninstalling whatever currently
    holds it before installing the next candidate -- and a single
    mechanism: only ever force a target *above* the current chamber temp
    (never a cooling demand), and let the human observer say what
    physically responded (heater, fridge, or nothing), since the
    firmware's function label doesn't need to match physical reality for
    the test to work. Normal and reversed polarity are now two automatic
    sub-tests per candidate, never a user-facing choice.
  * Fix: the pin-sweep could never succeed at all as previously
    sequenced -- it needs a live chamber-temperature reading to compute a
    baseline, but nothing was ever installed with the CHAMBER_TEMP
    function until the very last wizard step (finalize), which runs
    *after* the sweep. New install_probe action installs the identified
    chamber (and beer) probe immediately once confirmed in the wizard,
    not deferred to finalize.
  * Fix: identify_onewire_probes gave up after a fixed 90s and forced a
    manual "Try again" click, even though there's no hardware reason
    (unlike the relay sweep's real anti-short-cycle window) for this step
    to be time-boxed. It now polls until identified or the user cancels
    setup, with only a 3600s backstop against a truly orphaned task.
  * New: begin_device_config -- the wizard's first action -- snapshots
    the current installed-device state before wiping every device to
    unassigned, so a candidate pin is always tested from a genuinely
    blank slate. If a previous wizard session was abandoned mid-sweep
    (tab closed, no cancel), the *next* session self-heals by restoring
    that leftover snapshot first. reset_brewpi's cancel path now restores
    the same snapshot rather than just resetting -- a real "revert to the
    beginning state."
  * Fix: HardwareWizard.tsx's device-config stages could flash a false
    "no probe detected" for one render on every job-to-job transition
    (TanStack Query resets `data` to undefined the instant the polled
    test_id changes, before the new job's first response arrives) --
    confirmed live, reproduced on both the initial probe-identification
    entry and the chamber-to-beer phase transition. A genuinely failed
    sweep job (e.g. the chamber probe isn't reading) was also silently
    rendered as an ordinary "no cooling/heating seen" timeout, hiding the
    real error and inviting repeated identical failures across every
    remaining candidate -- now surfaced explicitly with the real error
    and a Retry action.
  * Fix: krauken/ipc/server.py's stop() hung for a full 90s
    (systemd's TimeoutStopSec) and got SIGKILLed on every
    krauken-manual/krauken-simulator restart, confirmed via real
    install timing -- Python 3.12 changed Server.wait_closed() to also
    wait for already-open client connections (e.g. the daemon's own
    persistent connection to these services), which never close on
    their own once idle. IPCServer now tracks and aborts open
    connections itself before waiting, restoring the pre-3.12 shutdown
    behavior.
  * Improved: krauken.postinst now echoes progress through each real
    step (venv creation, dependency install/skip, demo-seed trigger,
    eeptools build, service restart) -- the slow dependency-install step
    in particular had zero visible progress from apt/dpkg's own display,
    which looked identical to a hung install on a real Pi Zero W.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 13:12:52 -0400

krauken (0.1.6) stable; urgency=medium

  * New: Krauken now takes over BrewPi's own classic Device
    Configuration page entirely -- a guided wizard discovers which
    OneWire probe is chamber/beer and which pin drives cooling (and
    optional heating), then installs that mapping itself, so a user
    never needs BrewPi's separate web UI again. Replaces the previous
    confirm_heater/identify_probes-based setup flow, which silently
    assumed that mapping already existed via BrewPi's own UI -- an
    assumption confirmed false on the actual reference rig (a cooling
    actuator installed, no heat device installed anywhere). Everything
    below was verified against the real Arduino (firmware 0.2.13) and
    brewpi-remix/brewpi-firmware-rmx's actual source, not guessed.
  * New: platforms/brewpi/connection.py gains list_installed_devices/
    list_available_devices/list_all_devices/install_device/
    reset_and_reconnect, backed by the Arduino's 'd'/'h'/'U'/'R'
    commands. Installing a device requires an explicit, currently-
    unused slot number (0-15) -- confirmed via DeviceManager.cpp's
    source and a live write against real hardware that -1 (or omitting
    the slot) fails silently. A real firmware quirk found this session
    (an unrelated log message sometimes interleaves mid-array in a
    'd'/'h' response, splitting one JSON array across physical lines)
    is now handled explicitly rather than silently failing to parse.
  * New: platforms/brewpi/device_config.py's guided pin-sweep pays the
    real anti-short-cycle wait (up to 10 minutes, confirmed via
    TempControl.h's MIN_SWITCH_TIME/MIN_COOL_OFF_TIME_FRIDGE_CONSTANT)
    only once per session -- once any relay has genuinely engaged,
    reassigning the tested function to a different candidate pin
    engages the new one within one control-loop tick, since the
    firmware's own protection timers track the logical function, not
    which pin backs it (confirmed via DeviceManager.cpp). A positively-
    identified relay is deliberately left energized while the sweep
    continues for the other function -- the protection timers guard
    against rapid cycling, not continuous running -- and the final
    pushed configuration is followed by a real hardware reset, which
    deterministically forces every pin to a safe level via the
    firmware's own actuator-construction ordering.
  * New: platforms/registry.py's PlatformBinding gains a test_runners
    table (via the new PlatformTestRunner in contracts/interfaces.py)
    so a platform can own hardware-configuration test actions that
    don't fit the existing ChamberDriver/BeerTempSource/GravitySource
    Protocols, without teaching daemon/tests_runtime.py any
    platform-specific knowledge. confirm_heater/identify_probes/
    fire_outlet are unchanged -- they were already fully generic.
  * Fix: api/schemas.py's TestStartRequest.action was missing
    "confirm_heater" entirely -- a real API call to start it was
    rejected before ever reaching the daemon. Also adds the five new
    device-configuration actions.
  * Kept: confirm_heater survives as a standalone, narrower diagnostic
    (HardwareSetupView's "Test heater" button, shown once heating is
    already mapped) for "did my already-configured heater stop
    responding" re-verification -- it no longer drives initial setup.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 07:52:44 -0400

krauken (0.1.5) stable; urgency=high

  * Fix: platforms/simulator/plant.py's gravity_at() raised
    OverflowError ("math range error") for a sufficiently large
    positive elapsed-time input -- unlike every other math.exp() call
    in this module, whose exponents are unconditionally <= 0 by
    construction (see advance_physics's own docstring on a prior,
    similar incident), gravity_at()'s plain logistic formula needed a
    genuinely positive exponent past its midpoint, which overflows.
    Caught live on real hardware: a remapped-to-pure-simulator active
    fermentation, after a daemon restart onto the accelerated
    SimulatorClock, hit this on essentially the first control tick,
    and the control loop's own "one bad tick must never kill control"
    resilience then retried the identical failing read forever --
    with SimulatorClock never actually waiting between ticks, this
    pinned the Pi's single CPU core and produced >100 log lines/second
    indefinitely. Fixed with the standard numerically-stable sigmoid
    rewrite (only ever exponentiate the non-positive half, whichever
    side of the midpoint t_h is on) -- verified identical output to the
    original formula for every normal input, and that extreme t_h now
    correctly saturates at the curve's own asymptotes instead of
    raising. New tests/unit/test_simulator_plant.py (plant.py had no
    direct unit coverage at all before this).

